  "summary": "<p class=\"note\"><strong>From the calendar:</strong> it is not enough to know the trends. You must explain the reasoning using effective nuclear charge, core electrons, valence electrons, distance, attraction and repulsion.</p>\n  <p><strong>Video 13, F-block configuration.</strong> In the f block, the shell is the <strong>period minus two</strong>. Neodymium: [Xe]6s<sup>2</sup>4f<sup>4</sup>. The f block really belongs between the s and d blocks; it is printed below to save width.</p>\n  <p><strong>Videos 14 and 14.a, Para- and diamagnetism.</strong> Electrons are tiny magnets. Unpaired, parallel spins add up (<strong>paramagnetic</strong>, pulled into a magnetic field, shown by a balance that tips). Paired spins cancel (<strong>diamagnetic</strong>, weakly pushed out). He is diamagnetic, C and Na are paramagnetic, Na<sup>+</sup> is diamagnetic. Video 14.a has no transcript; from its title it applies this to N<sub>2</sub> and O<sub>2</sub>. The standard result: liquid O<sub>2</sub> is paramagnetic (two unpaired electrons) and N<sub>2</sub> is diamagnetic.</p>\n  <p><strong>Videos 15–17, Atomic and ionic radius.</strong> Atoms have no sharp edge, so radius is half the distance between two bonded nuclei. <strong>Across a period</strong> radius shrinks: protons are added but the number of core electrons doing the <strong>shielding</strong> stays the same, so the valence electrons are pulled in harder (Li vs. Be: both have 2 core electrons, but Be has 4 protons). Electrons in the same shell barely shield each other. <strong>Down a group</strong> radius grows because each period adds a shell. He is the smallest atom; the largest are at the bottom left. A <strong>cation</strong> is smaller than its atom (it loses its outer shell); an <strong>anion</strong> is larger. So Na<sup>+</sup> is smaller than Cl<sup>−</sup> even though the Na atom is bigger than the Cl atom. Na<sup>+</sup> is even a bit smaller than Ne (same electrons, one more proton).</p>\n  <p><strong>Videos 18–20, Ionization energy.</strong> The energy needed to remove an electron from a gaseous atom; always positive. It increases across a period and up a group, because Z<sub>eff</sub> = Z − S rises (Li ≈ +1, Be ≈ +2) and distance shrinks. Two dips: <strong>Be → B</strong> (900 → 800 kJ/mol) because boron’s electron is in a higher-energy 2p orbital, farther out and shielded by 2s; <strong>N → O</strong> (about 1400 → 1300) because oxygen’s fourth p electron is paired and repelled. For lithium, IE<sub>1</sub> = 520 but IE<sub>2</sub> = 7298 kJ/mol: the second electron is a 1s core electron, closer and unshielded. A huge jump in successive ionization energies shows how many valence electrons an atom has and which ion forms.</p>\n  <p><strong>Video 21, Electron affinity.</strong> The energy change when a gaseous atom gains an electron. A negative value means energy is released because the nucleus attracts the added electron (Li: −60 kJ/mol). It generally becomes more negative across a period (F releases the most). Exceptions: Be and N (the new electron goes into a higher 2p orbital or must pair) and Ne (it would start a new shell with Z<sub>eff</sub> ≈ 0), which have about zero or positive values. There is no reliable trend down a group.</p>",
